{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Maximum likelihood estimators (MLE) have properties that hold only when $n$ is much larger than 1 or as $n \\to \\infty$",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source [00:27:59\u201300:28:37]: 'All those properties hold true when $n$ is much larger than 1 and some of them even when $n$ goes to infinity.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Finite samples often lack sufficient observations to ensure the properties of MLE",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source [00:27:59\u201300:28:37]: 'In practice, however, we have to deal with the finite samples. And in many cases, the number of observations is not enough in order to ensure those four properties of maximum likelihood estimator.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "For a coin toss example, the MLE of the probability of heads is the observed frequency of heads ($\\hat{p} = k/n$)",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source [00:28:37\u201300:29:09]: 'How do you think what would be maximum likelihood estimation for this probability? ... KLM, of course. This is bridge retrieval. So just the frequency of the heads.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "With $n=2$ tosses and 2 heads, the MLE estimates the probability of heads as 1",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source [00:30:25\u201300:31:05]: 'So now let's assume that we have closed the coin just twice and we have observed heads in both trials. So then according to maximum value of the estimation, we should estimate the probability of a head equal to one.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The MLE result of $\\hat{p}=1$ contradicts common sense (a coin with two heads is unlikely)",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source [00:30:25\u201300:31:05]: 'Does it correspond to the common sense? Not really.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "With $n=1000$ tosses, the MLE $\\hat{p}=k/1000$ is likely a good estimate of the true probability of heads",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source [00:31:05\u201300:31:38]: 'If we performed say 1000 of trials, so if $N$ would be equal to 1000, then probably KON would be a good estimate for the true probability of a head.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Small sample sizes ($n \\ll \\infty$) cause MLE to fail in practice",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source [00:31:38\u201300:32:11]: 'So we've got some result of maximum likelihood estimation, but it contradicts even the concepts. But on the other hand, if we observed say, or if we performed say 1000 of trials, so if $N$ would be equal to 1000, then probably KON would be a good estimate for the true probability of a head, right? But not when $N$ equals to true.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The two-heads coin example demonstrates that MLE can produce results that contradict common sense when $n$ is small",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source [00:31:38\u201300:32:11]: 'This is a kind of a drawback of maximum likelihood estimation.'"
    }
  ],
  "clarity_1_to_5": 4,
  "caveat": "The ASR contains several mispronunciations/misstatements: 'KLM' (likely MLE), 'bridge retrieval' (likely maximum likelihood), 'KON' (likely MLE), 'N equals to true' (likely $n=2$), '1,000 viewers' (likely 1,000 dollars/bets). These are noted in the notes but may affect interpretation."
}
